## Introduction
Single-atom catalysts (SACs), as a new frontier of heterogeneous catalysis, have witnessed their great superiority in diverse reactions.[1-9] With the active sites dispersed at atomic level, SACs possess ultrahigh catalytic activity and selectivity and facilitate the understanding of the structure-performance relationship at atomic/molecular scale.[1-9] Generally, the catalytic performance of SACs is highly dependent on intrinsic properties of metal active sites and their surrounding microenvironment as well as physicochemical characteristics of supports, the latter of which pose great challenges to  the nature identification of catalytic sites.[10-12] Specifically, single-atom metal decorated Ndoped carbon $(M_{1}-N-C)$ materials, an important subclass of SACs, have been recognized as excellent electrocatalysts for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction reaction $(C O_{2}\mathsf{R R})$ ).[13-20] However, the carbon supports of the reported $M_{1}-N-C$ materials always display various characteristics (pore structure, surface area, morphology etc.), making the activity of $M_{1}$ -N-C tends to vary considerably even with the same metal center.[21,22] On account of this, the general synthesis of $M_{1}-N-C$ with a wide variety of metal species but similar microenvironment and supports is highly desired to identify the intrinsic activity of single-atom active sites.
Multivariate metal-organic frameworks (MTV-MOFs), an important subclass of MOFs incorporating multiple linkers of different functionalities within a single crystal, creating complexity within a skeleton in a controlled manner, have attracted growing attentions in many applications.[23-26] The diversity of constituent units in MTV-MOFs create huge opportunities for the general construction of $M_{1}-N-C$ with various metal centers. Meanwhile, it is ready for MTV-MOFs to control the variables of their derivatives, such as morphology, pore structure, surface area, elemental content, etc. Therefore, MTVMOFs are ideal candidates to construct SACs and exclusively identify the intrinsic activities of different metal centers for $C O_{2}\mathsf{R R}$ . Meanwhile, the porous feature of MOFs can be largely inherited to their derivatives, which will benefit the exposure of active sites and thereby $C O_{2}R R$ performance.[27-29]
In addition to the active site identification, another critical issue in $C O_{2}R R$ is the energy-consuming capture and purification process of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ . Specifically speaking, to achieve high selectivity, the currently reported $C O_{2}\mathsf{R R}$ are generally performed in pure $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ .[30-42] However, the actual concentration of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ feedstock available from industrial processes such as coal power plant (5- $15\%00_{2})$ and steel/petrochemical industry $14-33\%$ $C O_{2})$ is relatively low.[43-45] Given the thermodynamic stability of the $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}={\mathsf{O}}$ bond $(\sim806~\mathrm{\textkJ/mol)}$ of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ and its limited solubility in aqueous solution, low $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ concentration will significantly affect the activity, setting great barriers for the direct ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ utilization.[45-47] Therefore, the development of efficient electrocatalysts for direct conversion of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ at low pressures is extremely significant yet in its infancy and rarely achieved.[43,45]
With all above in mind, a series of isostructural porphyrinic MTV-MOFs constructed by M-TCPP $\langle{\mathsf{M}}={\mathsf{F}}{\mathsf{e}}$ , Co, Ni and Cu; ${\mathsf{T C P P}}=$ tetrakis (4-carboxyphenyl)porphyrin) and ${\sf H}_{2}$ -TCPP are selected as precursors. Thanks to the isomorphism of MTVMOFs and spatial isolation of metal centers in M-TCPP, four kinds of $M_{1}-N-C$ $(\mathsf{M}=\mathsf{F}\mathsf{e}$ , Co, Ni and Cu) materials, featuring single metal atoms with very similar carbon support environment, have been obtained upon pyrolysis and serve as model catalysts to compare the intrinsic activity of diverse single-atom metal species (Scheme 1). Among all four $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{M}}_{1}$ -N-C ( ${\sf M}={\sf F e}$ , Co, Ni and Cu) model catalysts for electrocatalytic $C O_{2}\mathsf{R R}$ , ${\mathsf{N i}}_{1}$ -N-C exhibits the highest CO Faradic efficiency (FE) of $96.8\%$ with a turnover frequency (TOF) up to $11315\ h^{-1}$ at $-0.8\ \vee$ in pure $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ . Theoretical calculations reveal that $N i_{1}-N-C$ possesses the most positive value of the limiting potential difference between $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction and ${\sf H}_{2}$ evolution among all four $M_{1}$ -N-C catalysts, elucidating its high $C O_{2}\mathsf{R R}$ selectivity in experiments. Remarkably, the best-performed $N i_{1}-N-C$ catalyst is able to catalyze the reduction of diluted $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , though a very challenging task, presenting excellent CO FE even at $30\%$ and $15\%$ $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ concentrations. This strongly proves the great superiority of $N_{1}$ - N-C toward selective $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction under practical conditions.
## Results and Discussion
Synthesis and characterization of M1-N-C.
A family of isostructural porphyrinic MTV-MOFs, named MPCN-222 $(\mathsf{M}\:=\:\mathsf{F e}$ , Co, Ni and Cu), have been successfully constructed by M-TCPP $\mathsf{M}=\mathsf{F e}$ , Co, Ni and Cu) and ${\sf H}_{2}$ -TCPP via the mixed ligand strategy (Figure S1).[28] With the general synthetic approach, the obtained M-PCN-222 involving different M-TCPP ligands present uniform rod-like shape with similar particle sizes, surface area and pore structure (Figure 1a, S2 and S3). Upon the pyrolysis of M-PCN-222 followed by $Z r O_{2}$ removal, $M_{1}-N-C$ ( ${\sf M}=$ Fe, Co, Ni and Cu) can be finally obtained. Taking $N i_{1}-N-C$ as a representative, it shows a rod-like shape with a diameter of ${\sim}200~\mathsf{n m}$ , similar to those of $\mathsf{F e}_{1}.$ , $\mathsf{C o}_{1}$ - and $\mathsf{C u}_{1}..\mathsf{N-C}$ (Figure 1b and S4). In addition, no obvious metal nanoparticle is found in the TEM images of $M_{1}-N-C$ (Figure 1b and S4). ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption measurements indicate that all $M_{1}-N-C$ possess similar surface area and pore size distribution (Figure S5, Table S2). Powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of all $M_{1}$ -
N-C present two broad peaks corresponding to the (002) and (101) planes of carbon and no peaks of metallic phase can be observed, in accordance with TEM results (Figure S6). In the Raman spectra of all $M_{1}-N-C$ catalysts, the intensity ratios of D band $(1345~\mathsf{c m}^{-1})$ ) and $\mathsf{G}$ band $(1590~\mathsf{c m}^{-1})$ are subject to small oscillation from 0.92 to 0.94, illustrating their similar graphitization degree (Figure S7, Table S2).
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was adopted to investigate the chemical compositions and electronic states of $M_{1}-N-C$ catalysts. The fitted N 1s XPS spectrum of $N i_{1}-N-C$ clearly shows five characteristic peaks including pyridinic N $(398.7~\mathsf{e V})$ , Ni-N moieties $(399.4~\mathsf{e V})$ , pyrrolic N $(400.2~\mathsf{e V})$ , graphitic N (401.1 eV) and oxidized N (402.6 eV) species (Figure S8a), confirming the existence of Ni-N bonding.[28,48] The Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ of $N i_{1}-N-C$ presents a binding energy $(855.4\:\Theta\mathsf{V})$ located between ${\sf N i}^{0}$ $(853.0\ \mathsf{e V})$ and ${\mathsf{N i}}^{2+}$ (855.7 eV), illustrating the partially oxidized Ni species originated from Ni-N species (Figure S9a).[19,49] Moreover, the N K-edge near-edge X-ray absorption fine structure (NEXAFS) analysis has been further conducted. For the metal-free N-doped carbon (N-C) derived from PCN-222 involving ${\sf H}_{2}$ -TCPP linker only, two $\pi^{\star}$ peaks corresponding to pyridinic N (peak a) and graphitic N (peak b), as well as a broaden peak (peak c) related to $\mathsf{C}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{N}\ \upsigma^{\star}$ bond, are detected (Figure S10a).[50,51] In contrast, peak a broadens and splits into double peaks ( $\mathtt{a}_{1}$ and a2) in $N i_{1}$ -N-C, suggesting the existence of Ni atoms stabilized by pyridinic N (Figure 2a).[51] Supportive results of XPS and N K-edge NEXAFS spectra are also obtained for $F e_{1}-$ , $\mathsf{C o}_{1}.$ - and $\mathsf{C u}_{1-}\mathsf{N}_{-}\mathsf{C}$ catalysts, all demonstrating the formation of corresponding M-N species (Figure S8, S9 and S10a). Quantitatively, the N content and Ni loading in $N i_{1}-N-C$ are determined to be 4.94 and $1.75\mathrm{wt\%}$ , similar to the other $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{M}}_{1}$ - N-C ( $\mathsf{M}=\mathsf{F e}$ , Co and Cu) catalysts (Table S2). In the aberrationcorrected high-angle annular dark field scanning transmission electron microscope (HAADF-STEM) images, all $M_{1}$ -N-C catalysts are featured with bright dots randomly dispersed in the selected area, clearly presenting the full of atomically dispersed metal species in the domain of each $M_{1}$ -N-C (Figure 1c-f).
Atomic structure analysis by X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS) studies.
To gain more information on the chemical environments of single metal atoms in $M_{1}-N-C$ , X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS) was carried out. Taking $N i_{1}-N-C$ as an example, the adsorption edge of $N i_{1}-N-C$ in the Ni-K edge XANES spectra locates between Ni foil and $N i_{2}O_{3}$ , manifesting the positive charge state of Ni in $N i_{1}-N-C$ , in consistence with the XPS result (Figure 2b and S9a). In the Fourier transform-extended X-ray absorption fine structure (FT-EXAFS) spectrum of $N i_{1}-N-C$ , only one dominated peak at $1.4\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ associated to the Ni-N scattering path is presented and no Ni-Ni bond $(\sim2.07\mathring{\mathsf{A}})$ can be observed, verifying the formation of singe-atom Ni sites in ${\sf N i}_{1}$ -N-C (Figure 2c). The EXAFS fitting suggests that the ${\mathsf{N i}}$ is coordinated by four N atoms (Figure 2d, Table S3). The XAS analyses have been performed on $\mathsf{F e}_{1}\cdot$ , $\mathsf{C o}_{1}.$ and $\mathsf{C u}_{1}-\mathsf{N-C}$ as well and the results illustrate that all $M_{1}-N-C$ possess similar metal coordination environment, featuring single-atom M coordinated by four N atoms (Figure S10 and S11, Table S3).
## Electrocatalytic performance for CO2RR in pure $\mathtt{c o}_{2}$
Encouraged by the above results, the $M_{1}$ -N-C materials indeed feature almost identical structures except for different single metal atom sites. Therefore, they are ideal model catalysts to decode the intrinsic properties of these single-atom metals, by deducting their similar microenvironment and support factors, in electrochemical $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction. As revealed by the linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves, $N i_{1}-N-C$ offers a much larger current response in pure ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ stream than the other $M_{1}$ -NC catalysts, indicating the significantly higher activity of $N i_{1}-N-C$ toward $C O_{2}\mathsf{R R}$ (Figure 3a and S12-S14). Furthermore, the Faradaic efficiencies (FEs) for $M_{1}-N-C$ catalysts at different potentials have been investigated. The $N i_{1}$ -N-C shows the best CO selectivity with a highest CO FE of $96.8\%$ at $-0.8{\mathrm{~V~}}$ among all $M_{1}$ -N-C catalysts (Figure 3b). Meanwhile, the high CO FE (over $90\%$ of $N i_{1}-N-C$ can be maintained in a wide potential range from $-0.65\mathrm{~V~}$ to -0.95 V with ${\sf H}_{2}$ as the only byproduct (no detectable liquid product), manifesting the excellent selectivity of $N i_{1}-N-C$ for $C O_{2}R R$ (Figure 3b, S15 and S16). The $F e_{1}-N-C$ catalyst is the second most active catalyst with a highest CO FE of $86.5\%$ . In comparison, the remaining two catalysts, $\mathsf{C o}_{1}..\mathsf{N}.\mathsf{C}$ and $\mathsf{C u}_{1}{-}\mathsf{N-C}$ , display much lower CO FE caused by the very competitive HER process (Figure 3b and S15). Moreover, the TOF and CO partial current density $(\mathsf{J}_{\mathsf{C O}})$ of $N i_{1}-N-C$ reach 11315 $\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ and $27\ \mathsf{m A}/\mathsf{c m}^{2}$ at $-0.8{\mathrm{~V~}}$ , far surpassing those of the other three $M_{1}$ -N-C catalysts (Figure 3c and S17). In fact, $N i_{1}-N-C$ shows a smaller Tafel slope (98 mV/decade) than that of $\mathsf{F e}_{1}.$ - (104 mV/decade), $\mathsf{C o}_{1^{-}}$ (142 mV/decade) and $\mathsf{C u}_{1}-\mathsf{N-C}$ (118 mV/decade), indicating the more favorable kinetics of ${\sf N i}_{1}$ -N-C. Meanwhile, $N i_{1}-N-C$ also shows smaller charge transfer resistance and larger electrochemical active surface area (ECSA) than other $M_{1}-N-C$ catalyts, further supporting its much higher catalytic activity (Figure S18 and S19). In addition, the best performed $N i_{1}-N-C$ exhibits a long-term stability at $-0.8\vee$ for $10\mathrm{~h~}$ without obvious decay in current density and FE of CO (Figure 3e). The distinctly different performance of $M_{1}-N-C$ model catalysts not only reveals that the catalytic activity and selectivity are strongly dependent on active metal species, but also unambiguously demonstrates that single-atom Ni site far exceeds the other metal sites in $M_{1}$ -N-C. This conclusion is of significant yet being achieved previously, given the complexity of different SACs with different structural parameters such as metal loading, surface area, pore structure, coordination environment, etc. For better comparison, the metal-free N-C prepared from the PCN-222, with similar structural characteristics as $M_{1}$ -N-C, has also been synthesized (Figure S1-S7, Table S1 and S2). It can be seen that N-C shows much lower current response, CO FE and turnover number (TON) and larger Tafel slope than ${\sf N i}_{1}$ - N-C for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction, further supporting the importance role of single Ni atoms (Figure 3b, 3d, S20 and S21). Furthermore, ${\sf N i}_{1}$ - N-C also shows better current response and CO FE than mix-NiN-C, the latter of which is derived from the mixture of Ni-TCPP and ${\sf H}_{2}$ -TCPP, further demonstrating the superiority of MOFs as precursors (Figure S22).
Theoretical Study of M1-N-C for $C O_{2}R R$ .
Electrocatalytic performance for the reduction of $\cos_{2}$ at low pressures.
On the basis of the experimental evidences and theoretical calculations above, $N i_{1}-N-C$ incorporated with $N i-N_{4}$ sites is highly efficient in selective $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction. Inspired by this, the electrocatalytic performance of $N i_{1}-N-C$ toward lowconcentration $\overline{{\mathsf{C O}_{2}}}$ reduction, which is a very challenging target in $C O_{2}\mathsf{R R}$ , has been further investigated. Prior to electrocatalytic measurements, the concentrations of protons $([H^{+}])$ and dissolved $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ $([\mathsf{C O}_{2}(\mathsf{a q})])$ in aqueous solution under different $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ concentrations are calculated in detail based on dissociation equilibrium theories and Henry’s law (Figure S26). Obviously, the $[H^{+}]$ and $[\mathsf{C O}_{2}(\mathsf{a q})]$ concentrations decrease significantly under lower ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ pressures, making $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction more difficult according to the reaction equilibrium $(\mathsf{C O}_{2}(\mathsf{a q})\ +$ $2H^{+}+2e^{-}\rightleftharpoons C O+H_{2}O)$ . Moreover, the equilibrium potentials are calculated based on the Nernst equation to be negatively shifted by 46 and $73~\mathsf{m V}$ when $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ partial pressure decreased from 1 atm to 0.3 and 0.15 atm (Figure S27). The calculations conclude that the $C O_{2}\mathsf{R R}$ will be much more difficult at low $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ partial pressures from the viewpoint of thermodynamics. Therefore, this raises a higher demand for both activity and selectivity of the electrocatalysts, toward addressing this challenging goal.
In the experimental results, the LSV curves of $N i_{1}-N-C$ in ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{A r}}$ mixed gas containing $30\%$ and $15\%$ $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reasonably present higher current responses than that in pure Ar, indicating the evident $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction activity under diluted $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ (Figure 5a). Using $30\%$ ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ as the feed gas, the maximal CO FE of ${\sf N i}_{1}$ -N-C reaches $91.6\%$ with $\mathsf{J}_{\mathsf{C O}}$ and TOF values of $6.69\ \mathsf{m A}/\mathsf{c m}^{2}$ and 1395 h-1 at $-0.8\ \vee$ (Figure 5b and S28). Even though the concentration of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ lowers to $15\%$ , a high CO FE $(83.2\%)$ can still be observed at $-0.75\mathrm{~V~}$ with $\mathsf{J}_{\mathsf{C O}}$ and TOF of $2.91\ \mathsf{m A}/\mathsf{c m}^{2}$ and $607~\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ (Figure 5b and S28). It can be seen that even though the CO partial current density goes down with decreased $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ concentration, the CO FE still reaches above $80\%$ under $15\%$ $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ (Figure 5b and S29). In addition to the main product of CO, $\mathsf{a}$ small amount of ${\sf H}_{2}$ as the only byproduct (no liquid product) can be detected at low $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ concentrations (Figure S30 and S16), similar to that in pure $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ . Furthermore, ${\mathsf{N i}}_{1}$ -N-C possesses stable current densities and CO FEs over $10\mathrm{~h~}$ at $30\%$ and $15\%$ $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ concentrations (Figure 5c). The results above clearly demonstrate the great potential of $N i_{1}-N-C$ for highly selective $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction by using dilute $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ feedstock from industrial processes.
## Conclusion
In summary, a series of single-atom catalysts $(M_{1}-N-C$ , ${\sf M}={\sf F e}$ , Co, Ni and Cu), exhibiting almost identical pore structures and composition except for different single metal atoms, have been rationally constructed from isostructural porphyrinic MTV-MOFs. The obtained $M_{1}$ -N-C, featuring very similar microenvironment and support effect for metal active sites, are employed as ideal model catalysts to identify the intrinsic properties of single-atom metal species in electrochemical $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction. Under pure $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ conditions, $N i_{1}-N-C$ , with single-atom Ni as the active center, manifests the most satisfactory $C O_{2}\mathsf{R R}$ performances with the highest CO selectivity up to $96.8\%$ , followed by $F e_{1}-N-C$ , then $\mathsf{C o}_{1}..\mathsf{N}.\mathsf{C}$ and $\mathsf{C u}_{1}-\mathsf{N-C}$ , among all investigated $M_{1}-N-C$ catalysts. DFT calculation results support the activity trend and illustrate that the best-performed $N i_{1}$ -N-C, with the most positive value of $\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{L}}(\mathsf{C O}_{2}){\mathsf{-U}}_{\mathsf{L}}(\mathsf{H}_{2})$ , can efficiently promote $C O_{2}\mathsf{R R}$ and suppress, to the maximum extent, the competitive HER. Given the ultrahigh selectivity of SACs, $N i_{1}-N-C$ has also been employed to catalyze the reduction of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ at low pressures, which is more challenging and rarely reported. Strikingly, the high selectivity of ${\sf N i}_{1}$ -N-C can be maintained even at $30\%$ and $15\%$ $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ concentrations, which are practical and directly available from industrial processes, further suggesting the superiority and ultrahigh selectivity of $N{\dot{\mathsf{I}}}_{1}$ - N-C for $C O_{2}R R$ . This work provides a general route to a broad class of model SACs, which eliminate the interference of microenvironment and support effect, unambiguously unveiling the superior intrinsic performance of single Ni atoms to other Fe, Co and Cu counterparts in $M_{1}-N-C$ for $C O_{2}\mathsf{R R}$ . Moreover, the preliminary results herein highlight the great potentials of Nibased SACs for the direct electrocatalytic $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ conversion at low concentrations in future practical applications.
